{#-
  WATCHER for one webCoRE `was_*` comparison.

  `was_less_than N for 10 minutes` asks "has this been continuously below N for
  the last 10 minutes". Home Assistant cannot answer that: the numeric_state
  CONDITION takes no `for:`, and `last_changed` resets on every update, so a
  sensor that reports while staying below its threshold never accumulates any
  time and the answer would be permanently false.

  So the compiler records the answer as it happens. This automation stamps the
  helper the moment the condition becomes true and clears it the moment it
  stops being true; the piston then just reads how long ago the stamp was.

  Both triggers are TEMPLATE triggers, which fire when their result goes from
  false to true — so this runs on the two flips only, NOT on every sensor
  update. That is what makes one of these per comparison affordable.

  This is HOME ASSISTANT SCHEMA — trigger kinds, the service names, the
  timestamp format — so it lives here rather than in Python. If HA renames any
  of it, this file is where a user fixes it with nobody upstream
  (COMPILER_SPEC "Jinja2 everywhere").

  Input:
    auto_id, alias   identity for the automation
    entity           the input_datetime holding "since when"
    predicate        Jinja that is true while the condition holds
    negated          the same Jinja, inverted
    sentinel         the value meaning "not currently true"
-#}
- id: {{ auto_id }}
  alias: "{{ alias }}"
  # `queued` so a flip cannot be lost: the two triggers can land back to back
  # when a value crosses and immediately re-crosses, and dropping either one
  # would leave the helper stamped with the wrong moment.
  mode: queued
  max: 10
  triggers:
    - trigger: template
      value_template: "{{ predicate }}"
      id: became_true
    - trigger: template
      value_template: "{{ negated }}"
      id: became_false
  actions:
    - choose:
        - conditions:
            - condition: trigger
              id: became_true
          sequence:
            - action: input_datetime.set_datetime
              target:
                entity_id: {{ entity }}
              data:
                datetime: "{{ '{{ now().strftime(\'%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S\') }}' }}"
        - conditions:
            - condition: trigger
              id: became_false
          sequence:
            - action: input_datetime.set_datetime
              target:
                entity_id: {{ entity }}
              data:
                datetime: "{{ sentinel }}"
